course: dictionary
lesson: 4257
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. Grate is one of these words. «Grate the cheese over the pasta» means rub cheese against a tool with sharp holes so it falls in tiny bits.
explain: Now read «The rusty grate covered the hole in the road.» The clues are hole and road, so grate here is a metal frame with gaps over an opening in the ground. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence best.
explain: Grate can also be a sound. «The scraping chair grates on my ears» means the noise is harsh and unpleasant. «It grates on me» means something annoys you again and again. Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence and checking that it still makes sense.
fact: Grate as a verb means to rub food into thin strips or tiny bits against a tool with sharp holes.
fact: Grate as a noun can mean a metal frame with gaps, such as one that holds coal in a fireplace or covers a drain.
fact: In the story, «a little square grating all running criss-cross» describes a frame made of crossed bars.
fact: Grate can also mean a harsh, unpleasant scraping sound, and «grates on your nerves» means it annoys you.
fact: Safety tip: keep your fingers away from the sharp side when you grate, and never drop things through a drain grate.
exercise: «Grate the carrot finely for the salad.» What does grate mean here? | To make a harsh sound | A metal frame over a drain | *To rub into thin strips or tiny bits | To shout loudly
exercise: «The rusty grate covered the hole in the road.» What is a grate here? | A sharp knife | A sudden loud shout | *A metal frame with gaps covering a hole | A cheese for pizza
exercise: Ella says, «The squeaky chalk grates on my nerves.» What does grates mean? | Is very bright | Is soft and sweet | Is far away | *Is harsh and annoys me
test: Leo says grate only ever means a metal frame. Is he right? | Yes, it only means a metal frame | *No, grate also means to rub food into tiny bits | Yes, it only means a sound | No, it means to dance
test: What does «grating» mean in «a little square grating all running criss-cross»? | A tasty snack | A soft cushion | *A frame made of crossed bars | A loud song
exam: Which sentence uses grate as a verb meaning to rub food? | «The grate in the fireplace was full of coal» | «Keep your fingers away from the grate» | «The grate covered the drain» | *«Please grate the cheese for the pizza»
